.TH GGE\-SAVES 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-saves \- writing, reading, versioning and organizing saves of GameState
.SH DESCRIPTION
save.h writes a pointer\-free struct to a slot file with a small header (magic, version, size) and reads it back only when all three match. Because GameState is designed to be pointer\-free (gge\-state(7)), saving the whole game is one call and loading is another; there is no serialization code to write or maintain.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
save_write (&e\->save, "slot1", g, sizeof *g, SAVE_VERSION);   /* true on success */
save_load  (&e\->save, "slot1", g, sizeof *g, SAVE_VERSION);   /* false: missing, wrong version, wrong size */
save_exists(&e\->save, "slot1");                                /* enable Continue */
save_delete(&e\->save, "slot1");                                /* permadeath, "erase save" */
.EE
.RE
.PP
.SH WHERE FILES GO
\fBEngine.save\fR is filled by engine_init from \fBEngineConfig.org\fR and \fB.app\fR: a per\-user folder such as \fB~/.local/share/<org>/<app>/\fR on Linux, \fB~/Library/Application Support/<org>/<app>/\fR on macOS, \fB%APPDATA%\e<org>\e<app>\e\fR on Windows, created if needed. With either NULL, files go to the current working directory, which is convenient during development (\fB./slot1.sav\fR next to the build). Slot names become file names (\fB<slot>.sav\fR); keep them to letters, digits and underscores.
.PP
.SH VERSIONING
\fBSAVE_VERSION\fR is a number you define (the \fBgge init\fR template puts it in game_state.h). Bump it every time GameState's layout changes: a field added, removed, reordered or resized, an array capacity changed, a nested struct changed. An older file then fails to load cleanly instead of being read into the wrong fields. The size check catches most layout changes even without a bump, but not a reorder of same\-sized fields, so bump anyway.
.PP
Migration (loading an old save into a new layout) is not provided. If it matters for a shipped game: keep the old struct definition under a different name, load with the old version and size into it, and copy field by field. For a game in development, deleting old saves is the right answer.
.PP
.SH WHEN TO SAVE
.TP
\fBCheckpoint / autosave\fR
on level start, on picking up a key item, on entering a safe room: \fBsave_write\fR from update.
.TP
\fBManual\fR
a Save item in the pause menu.
.TP
\fBOn quit\fR
from the title\-return path or engine_quit, but not from on_exit during engine_shutdown of a crashed state; prefer explicit points.
.TP
\fBContinuous (idle games)\fR
every N seconds from a Timer; a few hundred kilobytes is instant.
.SH LOADING INTO A RUNNING GAME
\fBsave_load\fR writes the bytes into \fB*g\fR and nothing else. Scene statics (camera, sprites, tilemap, menus) still describe the previous state, so load \fIbefore\fR entering the gameplay scene, or replace the scene afterwards so on_enter rebuilds them:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static void cont(Engine *e) {
  if (save_load(&e\->save, "slot1", e\->user, sizeof(GameState), SAVE_VERSION))
    engine_replace(e, game_scene());        /* on_enter rebuilds camera, map, sprites from g */
  else
    LOG("no valid save");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Loading from inside the gameplay scene (a quick\-load key) works if the scene then does what its on_enter does: reload the map for \fBg\->level\fR, snap the camera, reset \fBprev\fR positions.
.PP
.SH MULTIPLE SLOTS AND PROFILES
Slots are names: "slot1", "slot2", "auto", "profile_cal". A slot\-select menu calls save_exists for each and shows "empty" or a summary. For a summary without loading the whole file, keep a small header struct in its own slot ("slot1_meta": level, playtime, name) written alongside.
.PP
.SH WHAT A SAVE CONTAINS
Exactly \fBsizeof(GameState)\fR bytes after the header, laid out as this compiler laid them out. That is portable across runs of the same build on the same platform, which is what a game needs. It is not portable across compilers, architectures or struct changes; treat saves as belonging to one build. Floats and ints are stored raw; booleans as bytes; padding bytes are included (zero them by initializing with \fB(GameState){0}\fR if the file's contents matter for comparison).
.PP
.SH OPTIONS ARE STATE
Volume, difficulty, key bindings and other settings live in GameState so they save with everything else. Games that want settings independent of the play save keep a second, tiny struct in its own slot ("settings") with its own version.
.PP
.SH QUICK SAVE IN MEMORY
\fBstatic GameState snapshot; snapshot = *g; ... *g = snapshot;\fR is a save without the disk, useful for debugging, for "retry from checkpoint" without files, and for turn\-based undo (a small ring of snapshots). GameState must be small enough to copy freely for this to be cheap; under a megabyte, it is.
.PP
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
A pointer in GameState: it is saved as a number and points at garbage after load. The compiler cannot detect this; review the struct.
.IP \(bu 2
Forgetting the version bump: fields silently shift.
.IP \(bu 2
Saving from render: once per frame, forever.
.IP \(bu 2
Assuming save_write succeeded: a read\-only folder or a full disk returns false and logs; tell the player.
.IP \(bu 2
Loading and staying in the old scene: stale camera and map.
